Full Stack Web Development with Java Internship

Today’s session focused on advanced concepts of the Collections Framework, specifically the Map interface and the working of the Iterator interface.

🔹 Topics Covered
1️⃣ Map Interface

The Map interface stores data in key–value pairs.

Key points discussed:

Each key must be unique

Values can be duplicated

Stored in java.util package

Faster data retrieval using hashing mechanism

2️⃣ Hashing Concept

The mentor explained how HashMap internally uses hashing to store and retrieve elements efficiently.

Hash code generation

Bucket storage mechanism

Faster lookup performance

3️⃣ Map Implementations
✅ HashMap

Does not maintain insertion order

Allows one null key

Fast performance

✅ LinkedHashMap

Maintains insertion order

Slightly slower than HashMap

✅ TreeMap

Stores keys in sorted order

Does not allow null keys

Slower compared to HashMap

🔹 4️⃣ Iterator Interface

The session covered the Iterator interface used to traverse collections.

Important methods:

hasNext() → Checks if more elements are present

next() → Returns the next element

remove() → Removes the current element

🔹 Fail-Fast Behavior

Iterator throws ConcurrentModificationException

Happens when collection is modified while iterating

Ensures safe traversal of data

